WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
Let's talk about practice for loops.

00:04.000 --> 00:08.000
Going down to control statements on looping,

00:08.000 --> 00:11.000
we talked about if else and else if.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.000
So when we have a statement like this,

00:15.000 --> 00:19.000
the if statement we're going to check and see if the expression afterwards is true.

00:19.000 --> 00:21.000
If it is, we will run this code.

00:21.000 --> 00:25.000
If there's an if present, we will keep

00:26.000 --> 00:28.000
and the if statement is false.

00:28.000 --> 00:33.000
We will keep checking the else ifs until we find one that is true.

00:33.000 --> 00:37.000
If there are no else ifs with a true statement,

00:37.000 --> 00:41.000
then we will jump to the else and go through the code here.

00:41.000 --> 00:45.000
So in this case, when we call this on the number five,

00:45.000 --> 00:48.000
we're going to print out this is a small number.

00:48.000 --> 00:55.000
For 99, we're going to say this.

00:55.000 --> 00:57.000
This is a pretty small number as well.

00:57.000 --> 01:01.000
If it's exactly 100, we're going to say this number is all right.

01:01.000 --> 01:02.000
And then for this number here,

01:02.000 --> 01:06.000
we will jump to the else statement and say this number is huge.

01:06.000 --> 01:09.000
We can also have multiple conditions in an if statement.

01:09.000 --> 01:15.000
So here, we are checking to see if the number that is passed into fizz buzz is both.

01:15.000 --> 01:21.000
Divisible by three and divisible by five, then we'll print fizz buzz.

01:21.000 --> 01:24.000
So we print nothing for three, nothing for five,

01:24.000 --> 01:28.000
and then fizz buzz for the number 15.

01:28.000 --> 01:32.000
We also can use if to test for an empty list.

01:32.000 --> 01:35.000
So here, my function is expecting a list.

01:35.000 --> 01:39.000
Remember that an empty list is false and results to false.

01:39.000 --> 01:43.000
While a list with values in it is truthy and will

01:43.000 --> 01:45.000
resolve to true.

01:45.000 --> 01:49.000
So when I pass in a list with values, this if statement will be valid,

01:49.000 --> 01:52.000
and I'll jump down into this block of code.

01:52.000 --> 01:59.000
If I pass in an empty list, I'm going to jump straight to the else statement here

01:59.000 --> 02:02.000
and print out got an empty list.

02:02.000 --> 02:08.000
We talked about some of those helper functions like the range and a numerate.

02:08.000 --> 02:12.000
We can use those in conjunction with the four loop as well.

02:12.000 --> 02:18.000
If I have a list defined here, if I wanted to go through all the values between zero and two,

02:18.000 --> 02:22.000
I could write them out manually and have a bad time of it.

02:22.000 --> 02:26.000
Or instead, I can use a shortcut of the range function.

02:26.000 --> 02:31.000
Pass in a start of zero and an end of three because that ending value is not inclusive.

02:31.000 --> 02:33.000
And do the same thing.

02:33.000 --> 02:38.000
The advantage of using range is that there are some optimizations in play that mean

02:38.000 --> 02:41.000
I don't have to define a list with all my values in it.

02:42.000 --> 02:49.000
Which is fine for my list of three items, but it starts to become more of an issue for large or complex lists.

02:49.000 --> 02:57.000
And lastly, if we wanted to loop over and have both an index and the value at that index,

02:57.000 --> 03:00.000
we want to use the enumerate function.

03:00.000 --> 03:08.000
And the enumerate function is a generator, but if you take a look at what it's actually returning,

03:08.000 --> 03:11.000
a list of two poles.

03:11.000 --> 03:16.000
And the first item in that two pole is an index, and the second is the item itself.

03:16.000 --> 03:21.000
So we can use two pole unpacking in our four statement here,

03:21.000 --> 03:26.000
and get back the index and the item from enumerate all in one step.

03:26.000 --> 03:30.000
We can also use this to loop over a dictionary.

03:30.000 --> 03:36.000
Remember, by default, if you loop over a dictionary, you loop over just the keys.

03:36.000 --> 03:44.000
If you want both the keys and the values, you have to make sure that you call the dot items function on it.

03:44.000 --> 03:55.000
And when you do that, you also get back a list of two poles with the first value being the key and the second value being the value itself.

03:55.000 --> 04:03.000
So you can also use two pole unpacking here to loop through both the key and the value at the same time.

04:03.000 --> 04:11.000
We can use return statements to break out of functions and break out of loops, which is useful when you are looking for a flag.

04:11.000 --> 04:18.000
And I'm not going to go over while loops, but if you want to learn more about while loops or nested loops,

04:18.000 --> 04:23.000
I recommend that you read the chapters at practical.learnpython.dev.

04:23.000 --> 04:29.000
I also recommend that you check out the optional chapter on break and continue on your own time,

04:29.000 --> 04:35.000
because those are two concepts that tend to trip out folks who are beginners or intermediate programmers.

04:35.000 --> 04:39.000
And they're important concepts for you to have a solid grasp of.

